// The development workspace: one checkout, four modules.
//
// `packages/` is the shared module both processes import, and each app has its
// own go.mod because each is its own binary with its own dependency graph — the
// gateway carries whatsmeow and no Temporal client, the backend the reverse, and
// the virtual computer's control plane carries nothing but the standard library.
//
// A workspace is a convenience for a checkout, not a build input: a Docker build
// does not read this file, which is why every go.mod also carries a `replace`
// pointing at the sibling copy of `packages/`. The two mechanisms say the same
// thing in the two places a build happens. The control plane has no `replace`
// because it imports nothing outside its own module.

go 1.26.0
use (
	./apps/backend
	./apps/virtual-computer/control
	./apps/whatsapp-gw
	./packages
)
